     8	
     9	Project skill for every agent writing or reviewing a Claude Code hook in the gobbi codebase. Loaded at Study phase when a task touches `.claude/hooks/` or asks for guidance on hook design.
    10	
    11	Two in-tree witnesses ground every rule below. Read them fully before any hook work:
    12	
    13	- `.claude/hooks/session-start.sh` (79 lines) — SessionStart event; reads stdin JSON and appends `export VAR=value` lines to `$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE` for downstream Bash tool calls and subagents.
    14	- `.claude/hooks/post-tool-use-agents.sh` (251 lines) — PostToolUse and PostToolUseFailure events; upserts an `agents[]` entry in `session.json` for every spawned Task/Agent tool call, using `flock -x` serialization and atomic `mv`.
    15	
    16	---
    17	
    18	## When to load
    19	
    20	- When editing any file under `.claude/hooks/`
    21	- When constructing a delegation prompt for an executor whose task creates or modifies a hook
    22	- When evaluating a hook-authoring artifact
    23	- When the task description references hook registration, stdin JSON payload, or `session.json` upsert patterns
    24	
    25	---
    26	
    27	## Core Principles
    28	
    29	> **A hook that blocks Claude is worse than a missed record.**
    30	
    31	PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure hooks MUST always exit 0. Diagnostics go to stderr; the script must never abort the tool call it is hooked to. Use a `bail()` function that logs and `exit 0`s gracefully. The `session-start.sh` is the exception — it exits 1 only if `$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE` is unset or unwritable, because those conditions make the hook entirely useless (nothing can be exported).
    32	
    33	> **Strict mode, but scope it correctly.**
    34	
    35	`session-start.sh` uses `set -euo pipefail` because any failure in the env passthrough is fatal — there is no meaningful fallback. `post-tool-use-agents.sh` uses `set -uo pipefail` but NOT `-e`, because individual extraction steps may legitimately produce empty strings or non-zero exits that should not abort the whole hook.
    36	
    37	> **jq -r @sh for every JSON value written to a shell file.**
    38	
    39	When writing `export VAR=value` lines to `$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE`, always serialize via `jq -r '@sh "export VAR=\(.field)"'`. The `@sh` format produces POSIX single-quote quoting that is safe against paths with spaces, shell metacharacters, empty strings, single quotes, and Unicode. Never use raw string concatenation or printf for this purpose.
    40	
    41	> **flock -x serializes every read-modify-write on shared JSON files.**
    42	
    43	Any hook that reads, modifies, and writes a shared file (e.g., `session.json`) must hold an exclusive flock for the entire critical section. Release is automatic when the subshell exits. Validate the output JSON before the atomic `mv`; if validation fails, delete the tmp file and `exit 0` (do not replace the original with garbage).
    44	
    45	> **Tool-name scope filter before any real work.**
    46	
    47	Hooks registered for broad events (PostToolUse fires on every tool) MUST filter to the specific tool(s) they care about as the very first substantive check after stdin parsing. Use a `case` statement; unrecognized tool names must `exit 0` cleanly, not fall through to the hook logic.
    48	
    49	---
    50	
    51	## Procedures
    52	
    53	### P1 — Hook event registration in `.claude/settings.json`
    54	
    55	Register every hook under `hooks.<EventName>[]` in `.claude/settings.json`. Each entry must have:
    56	
    57	- `matcher`: a regex string matched against `tool_name` (for PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure) or `source` (for SessionStart). Use `|` for alternatives: `"Task|Agent"` matches both Task and Agent tool names. For SessionStart, the matcher is matched against `hook_event_name.source`; the current project uses `"startup|resume|clear|compact"` to cover all SessionStart triggers.
    58	- `hooks[].command`: the shell command that runs the hook script, e.g. `"bash .claude/hooks/session-start.sh"`.
    59	
    60	Both PostToolUse and PostToolUseFailure entries can point to the same script (as `post-tool-use-agents.sh` does) — the hook reads `hook_event_name` from stdin to distinguish the event type.
    61	
    62	Example shape (from project settings):
    63	```json
    64	"PostToolUse": [
    65	  {
    66	    "matcher": "Task|Agent",
    67	    "hooks": [{ "command": "bash .claude/hooks/post-tool-use-agents.sh" }]
    68	  }
    69	],
    70	"PostToolUseFailure": [
    71	  {
    72	    "matcher": "Task|Agent",
    73	    "hooks": [{ "command": "bash .claude/hooks/post-tool-use-agents.sh" }]
    74	  }
    75	]
    76	```
    77	
    78	### P2 — stdin JSON payload structure
    79	
    80	Claude Code pipes a JSON object to stdin on every hook event. Always read it with `payload="$(cat)"` at the top of the script, then parse with `jq`. Never stream stdin directly into multiple `jq` calls — read once, use `<<<"$payload"` for all subsequent extractions.
    81	
    82	Common fields present across all events:
    83	- `session_id` — the current session UUID
    84	- `cwd` — the working directory at hook invocation
    85	- `hook_event_name` — the event name string (e.g., `"PostToolUse"`)
    86	- `transcript_path` — path to the session transcript JSONL
    87	
    88	PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure also include:
    89	- `tool_name` — name of the tool that was used (e.g., `"Task"`, `"Agent"`)
    90	- `tool_use_id` — UUID that correlates the tool call across hook events and transcript entries
    91	- `tool_input` — the full tool input object (for Task/Agent: includes `prompt`, `model`, `subagent_type`)
    92	- `tool_result` — the canonical result object (PostToolUse only)
    93	
    94	SessionStart includes:
    95	- `source` — one of `startup`, `resume`, `clear`, `compact`
    96	- `agent_id`, `agent_type`, `permission_mode` — optional fields, null when absent
    97	
    98	### P3 — env-file passthrough pattern (from session-start.sh)
    99	
   100	The env-file passthrough writes shell-safe `export VAR=value` lines to `$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE` so downstream Bash tool calls and subagents can source the file to pick up the exported values.
   101	
   102	Three tiers:
   103	
   104	**REQUIRED fields** — always present in the payload; emit unconditionally:
   105	```bash
   106	jq -r '@sh "export CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID=\(.session_id)"' <<<"$payload" >> "${CLAUDE_ENV_FILE}"
   107	```
   108	
   109	**OPTIONAL fields** — may be null; emit only when non-null:
   110	```bash
   111	jq -r 'if .agent_id != null then @sh "export CLAUDE_AGENT_ID=\(.agent_id)" else empty end' \
   112	    <<<"$payload" >> "${CLAUDE_ENV_FILE}"
   113	```
   114	
   115	**PASSTHROUGH re-exports** — re-export if already set in this process's environment (use `%q` quoting, which is bash-only — safe because the shebang is `#!/usr/bin/env bash`):
   116	```bash
   117	for _var in CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA; do
   118	    if [[ -n "${!_var:-}" ]]; then
   119	        printf 'export %s=%q\n' "${_var}" "${!_var}" >> "${CLAUDE_ENV_FILE}"
   120	    fi
   121	done
   122	```
   123	
   124	Note: `session-start.sh` exports `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` from the payload's `session_id` field. In a hook context, `$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` refers to the session Claude Code is running — the parent session for a manager hook, the subagent session for a subagent hook. This is hook-mechanics documentation; do not confuse it with the delegation-prompt `session-id:` field used for file-path construction in agent skills.
   125	
   126	### P4 — agents[] upsert pattern with flock (from post-tool-use-agents.sh)
   127	
   128	The critical section for a read-modify-write on `session.json`:
   129	
   130	```bash
   131	lock_file="$session_json.lock"
   132	tmp_file="$session_json.tmp.$$"
   133	
   134	(
   135	    flock -x 9 || { log "flock failed on $lock_file"; exit 0; }
   136	
   137	    # jq pipeline: upsert by id; atomic mv
   138	    if ! jq --argjson new "$upsert_input" '
   139	        .agents = (
   140	            (.agents // [])
   141	            | (map(.id) | index($new.id)) as $idx
   142	            | if $idx == null
   143	              then . + [ $new + { startedAt: ($new.finishedAt) } ]
   144	              else (.[$idx] | (.startedAt // $new.finishedAt)) as $kept_started
   145	                   | .[0:$idx]
   146	                     + [ .[$idx] + $new + { startedAt: $kept_started } ]
   147	                     + .[$idx+1:]
   148	              end
   149	        )
   150	    ' "$session_json" > "$tmp_file"; then
   151	        log "jq upsert failed"
   152	        rm -f "$tmp_file"
   153	        exit 0
   154	    fi
   155	
   156	    if ! jq -e . "$tmp_file" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
   157	        log "tmp file failed JSON validation"
   158	        rm -f "$tmp_file"
   159	        exit 0
   160	    fi
   161	
   162	    mv -f "$tmp_file" "$session_json"
   163	) 9>"$lock_file"
   164	```
   165	
   166	Key points:
   167	- The `(...)  9>"$lock_file"` subshell holds the lock for the entire read-modify-write.
   168	- `flock -x 9` blocks until the lock is acquired; the `|| { log ...; exit 0; }` guard handles the rare case where flock itself fails.
   169	- Always validate the tmp file with `jq -e .` before `mv` — never replace the original with invalid JSON.
   170	- Use `$$.` in the tmp filename to make it process-unique and avoid collisions from concurrent hook invocations.
   171	
   172	### P5 — two-tier extraction (from post-tool-use-agents.sh)
   173	
   174	When extracting rich result data, prefer the `toolUseResult` from the transcript JSONL (tier 1 — richer, includes `agentId`, `agentType`, `usage`, `totalDurationMs`) and fall back to the `tool_result` from stdin (tier 2 — canonical but leaner):
   175	
   176	```bash
   177	tier1=""
   178	if [[ -n "$transcript_path" && -f "$transcript_path" ]]; then
   179	    tier1=$(jq -c --arg tuid "$tool_use_id" '
   180	        select(.toolUseResult != null)
   181	        | select((.message.content[]?.tool_use_id // empty) == $tuid)
   182	    ' "$transcript_path" 2>/dev/null | tail -n1 || true)
   183	fi
   184	```
   185	
   186	Use `tail -n1` to pick the last matching line — retries supersede earlier attempts. Fall back to `jq -c '.tool_result // null' <<<"$payload"` when tier 1 is empty.
   187	
   188	### P6 — session-dir resolver pattern
   189	
   190	When the hook needs to locate `session.json` given `session_id` and `cwd`, use a two-step resolver:
   191	
   192	1. **Step (i) DORMANT**: read `$cwd/.gobbi/project.json` if it exists; extract `.name`.
   193	2. **Step (ii) ACTIVE**: scan `$cwd/.gobbi/projects/` — if exactly one project directory exists, use its basename. If zero or more than one, `bail`.
   194	
   195	Then scan `$project_dir/sessions/` for a directory whose suffix matches the `session_id` UUID. Require exactly one match; bail if ambiguous.
   196	
   197	### P7 — testing and verification of a hook
   198	
   199	Before merging any hook change:
   200	
   201	1. Run the hook manually with a minimal valid stdin payload: `echo '{"session_id":"test","cwd":"/tmp",...}' | bash .claude/hooks/your-hook.sh`. Confirm it exits 0 and produces the expected output.
   202	2. Verify the hook is registered in `.claude/settings.json` under the correct event and matcher.
   203	3. Start a Claude Code session and trigger the event. Inspect the output file or `session.json` to confirm the hook fired.
   204	4. Test the failure path: pass malformed JSON to stdin, verify the hook exits 0 and logs to stderr without corrupting output files.
   205	5. For `flock`-based hooks: confirm concurrent invocations (two background processes simultaneously) produce a valid merged result.
   206	
   207	---
   208	
   209	## Constraints
   210	
   211	- **MUST always exit 0 in PostToolUse and PostToolUseFailure hooks** — a non-zero exit blocks the tool call that triggered the hook. Log diagnostics to stderr; never abort the hook for recoverable conditions.
   212	- **MUST use `jq -r @sh`** for writing JSON string values into shell `export` lines — never raw string interpolation or printf without quoting.
   213	- **MUST hold `flock -x` for the entire read-modify-write critical section** — acquire the lock at the top of the subshell; release is automatic at subshell exit.
   214	- **MUST validate the tmp file with `jq -e .` before `mv`** — replacing a valid JSON file with invalid JSON is unrecoverable without manual intervention.
   215	- **MUST filter by tool_name** before executing hook logic in PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure hooks — use a `case` statement; all non-matching tool names exit 0 immediately.
   216	- **MUST use `bail()`** (log to stderr + `exit 0`) for every error condition in PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure hooks — never `exit 1` from these events.
   217	- **NEVER use `-e` in `set -euo pipefail`** for PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure hooks — individual `jq` extractions may legitimately fail; `-e` would abort the whole hook on a missing optional field.
   218	- **NEVER stream stdin to multiple `jq` calls** — read `payload="$(cat)"` once at the top; use `<<<"$payload"` for all subsequent `jq` invocations.
   219	- **NEVER write to `$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE` without first checking it is set and writable** — the guard at the top of `session-start.sh` (lines 32-39) is the required pattern.
   220	- **ALWAYS register the hook in `.claude/settings.json`** under the correct event name and matcher — an unregistered hook never fires.
   221	
   222	---
   223	
   224	## Anti-patterns
   225	
   226	- **Forgetting the tool-name filter** — registering a PostToolUse hook for a broad matcher without the `case "$tool_name" in ... esac` filter causes the hook to run on every tool invocation (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, ...) rather than only Task/Agent spawns. Add the filter immediately after stdin parsing.
   227	
   228	- **Raw string quoting in env-file exports** — writing `echo "export MYVAR=$value" >> "$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE"` is fragile if `$value` contains spaces, dollar signs, or single quotes. Always use `jq -r '@sh'` for JSON-sourced values; use `printf '%q'` for values sourced from the shell environment.
   229	
   230	- **Non-atomic file replacement** — writing directly to `session.json` without a tmp-file `mv` risks a partial write if the hook is interrupted mid-write. Always write to a tmp file, validate, then `mv -f`.
   231	
   232	- **Using `set -e` in PostToolUse hooks** — a single non-zero exit from a `jq` extraction (e.g., a missing optional field returns 1 even with `// null`) will abort the hook mid-script and produce an unpredictable partial state. Omit `-e`; use explicit `|| true` on fallible extractions.
   233	
   234	- **Hardcoding session paths** — never hardcode the session UUID or session directory path in hook scripts. Always derive the session directory by resolving from `session_id` + `cwd` via the two-step resolver pattern (P6).
   235	
   236	---
   237	
   238	## Output paths
   239	
   240	Hooks write to files at runtime; the paths below are the live runtime targets, not session staging paths.
   241	
   242	| Output | Path | Written by | Event |
   243	|---|---|---|---|
   244	| Env-var export lines | `$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE` (runtime env; path set by Claude Code) | `session-start.sh` | SessionStart |
   245	| agents[] entry in session.json | `.gobbi/projects/{project}/sessions/{date}-{uuid}/session.json` | `post-tool-use-agents.sh` | PostToolUse / PostToolUseFailure |
   246	
   247	Hook scripts themselves live at:
   248	
   249	| File | Event | Lines |
   250	|---|---|---|
   251	| `.claude/hooks/session-start.sh` | SessionStart | 79 |
   252	| `.claude/hooks/post-tool-use-agents.sh` | PostToolUse + PostToolUseFailure | 251 |
   253	
   254	Registration is in `.claude/settings.json` under the `hooks` key, one array entry per event.

